# What this repository publishes as a book collection.
# Generated by bin/pb-config --init on 2026-09-08, then decided by jp-tools.
# Each line below is a choice with a reason; none of it is a default.

id    = jp-tools
title = JP_TOOLS: project notes

book      = README=README.md
books_dir = docs

# CODE: DECLARED, and the two things that decided it were checked, not assumed.
#   1. gen_code builds "the repository's TRACKED source", so it is git-based
#      rather than a filesystem walk. This repo tracks 66 files and ZERO under
#      vendor/, which exists on disk and is gitignored. A walk would have swept
#      hundreds of third-party files; a tracked build does not. That was the
#      blocker and it is answered.
#   2. It is scrubbed like every other file source, for Joe's stated reason --
#      "the code is the thing that exposes the most secrets if it walks off".
#      The accepted cost is real and stated in gen_code: a scrubbed CODE book no
#      longer matches the code byte for byte.
# The benefit is the reason this repo of all repos should have it: JP_TOOLS is a
# toolbox, its substance IS the tools, and METHODOLOGY.md 2.10 now requires
# searching for prior art before building anything. A tool nobody can find gets
# rebuilt. That happened here on 2026-08-14: spool-audit.py grew a --fix half
# that reimplemented `cancel -a -x`.
code = yes

# TRANSCRIPTS: DECLARED NONE, deliberately (jp-tools, 2026-09-08).
#   Large, and the value is unclear for a repo whose reasoning already lands in
#   METHODOLOGY.md, docs/ and the fleet's session reports. "Always scrubbed" is
#   a property I have not verified for this repo's transcripts, and declaring an
#   exposure on the strength of someone else's measurement is the borrowed
#   description this project spent 2026-09-08 documenting. Revisit with a reason.
#   The key is present and empty so the fleet's config refresh reads it as a
#   decision rather than an omission; the fleet corpus carries this repo's
#   transcripts from the backup regardless (projectbook, 2026-09-12).
transcripts =

# ISSUES: joe/JP_TOOLS on Forgejo (jp-tools, 2026-09-14).
#   The tracker moved to Forgejo on Joe's decision ("move jp_tools tracker to
#   forgejo"): all 61 issues and PRs imported with their numbers and states,
#   verified against the original item for item. vm/issues-book.py reads
#   Forgejo, so this slug is exactly what it fetches. The open issues are the
#   prior art the code declaration above exists to serve. It needs the network:
#   build with FORGEJO_TOKEN set, or the book is a named skip.
issues = joe/JP_TOOLS

# What changed and when. HISTORY is `--stat`, DIFFS is `-p`.
history = yes
diffs = yes
